{% markdown %}

[Datatype](https://franktisellano.github.io/datatype/), by Frank Tisellano, is a variable font that turns text into charts. Write `{b:30,70,50,90}` in any element and it renders an inline bar chart through OpenType ligature substitution. There are also sparklines (`{l:...}`) and pie charts (`{p:...}`), with weight and width axes adjusting density and visual weight.

It caught my eye partly because of the name. Back in 2018, at EMBL-EBI, I wrote [What if: A web font for data](/posts/20180319-data-font-for-life-sciences.html). My version of a "data font" was the opposite of this one: a font designed for *reading* dense scientific data, where rows of identifiers like `ENSMUST00000032717` are the daily work. I was sketching monospace shapes, ligature treatments for repeated zeros, alternates for ambiguous characters: a font as a better tool for the data's *reader*.

Datatype is also a data font, but it's inside out. The text isn't there to be read in the normal sense; it's there to be drawn. The font carries the chart, the chart is the typesetting, and what you write in the HTML is essentially the data itself. My old framing asked how a font could help us *see through* data; Datatype asks how a font can *be* the data.

## How you use it

Here's what that looks like in code: load the font, apply it to an element, write the chart syntax as content.

{% endmarkdown %}

{% codeAndDemo 'html' %}
<style>
  /* Grab the font from https://fonts.google.com/specimen/Datatype, then: */
  .chart {
    font-family: 'Datatype', sans-serif;
    font-feature-settings: 'liga' 1, 'calt' 1;
    font-size: 1.4em;
  }
</style>

<p>Quarterly sales: <span class="chart">{b:40,75,60,90}</span></p>
<p>Pageviews this week: <span class="chart">{l:30,45,40,60,55,80,70}</span></p>
<p>Disk used: <span class="chart">{p:62}</span></p>
{% endcodeAndDemo %}

{% markdown %}

The text in the curly braces really is just text in the DOM. The browser's text engine handles the rest; nothing else has to initialise.

## Datatype on real data: my own blog cadence

For real numbers, here's the cadence of this blog rendered through Datatype. If the font fails to load, you'll see the raw expressions instead, which is itself a useful preview of the fallback behavior.

{% endmarkdown %}

<div class="kh-datatype">
  <div class="kh-datatype-row">
    <span class="kh-datatype-row__label" id="kh-chart-bar-label">Posts per year, 2017–2026 (bar)</span>
    <span class="kh-datatype-row__chart" role="img" aria-labelledby="kh-chart-bar-label" aria-description="2017: 6 posts, 2018: 7, 2019: 4, 2020: 4, 2021: 5, 2022 through 2024: zero, 2025: 34, 2026 to date: 32">
      <span aria-hidden="true">{b:18,21,12,12,15,0,0,0,100,94}</span>
    </span>
  </div>
  <div class="kh-datatype-row">
    <span class="kh-datatype-row__label" id="kh-chart-line-label">Digesting cadence by month, Nov 2025 – May 2026 (sparkline)</span>
    <span class="kh-datatype-row__chart" role="img" aria-labelledby="kh-chart-line-label" aria-description="Monthly digesting posts: 1, 2, 0, 1, 4, 3, 2">
      <span aria-hidden="true">{l:25,50,0,25,100,75,50}</span>
    </span>
  </div>
  <div class="kh-datatype-row">
    <span class="kh-datatype-row__label" id="kh-chart-pie-label">Share of all posts that are digesting entries (pie)</span>
    <span class="kh-datatype-row__chart" role="img" aria-labelledby="kh-chart-pie-label" aria-description="13 percent of posts are digesting entries (13 of 97)">
