  </div>
</div>

{% markdown %}

The bars show a steady trickle through the late 2010s, silence in 2022 to 2024, and a sharp return in 2025. The sparkline puts digesting entries at roughly two a month since I started them. The pie says these short-form notes are still a small slice of what I publish.

## Where it gets fragile

Cool as it is, I'd think hard before reaching for this in production. Screen readers will read the raw `{b:...}` text aloud unless you give each chart a real text alternative; the markup above uses `role="img"` with `aria-description` on the container and `aria-hidden` on the inner expression, but that's belt-and-braces work the font itself can't do. Copy-paste through editors that normalize curly braces or strip ligatures will silently break the syntax. And if the font fails to load, the raw expressions show through, which rhymes with [Miriam Suzanne's argument about combining CSS units](/posts/20250927-the-best-css-unit-might-be-a-combination/): the platform usually has a primitive already, and the trick is giving it room to degrade.

Honestly, for most real charts I'd reach for, a small SVG or a tiny helper would probably be simpler and more reliable than a font with a custom syntax. But that's not really the point. It's a nice thing to look at an idea you spent time on years ago and see someone walking the same name down the opposite road, and do it well.

If you've found a real use for it, [send me a note](mailto:khawkins98@gmail.com). I'd like to see where it lands.

